<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reting Rinpoche</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Thubten Jampal Yeshe Gyaltsen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Reting Rinpoche, the fifth holder of the title, Thubten Jampal Yeshe Gyaltsen, was regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> from 1934 to 1941. He led the search that identified the Fourteenth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> and presided over his enthronement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> in 1940. After resigning in favour of Taktra Rinpoche, he was accused of plotting to return to power and died in prison in Lhasa in 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reting-rinpoche.jpg" alt="Reting Rinpoche" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ernst Schäfer · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_135-S-12-40-25,_Tibetexpedition,_Regent_von_Tibet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Reting Rinpoche is the title of the abbots of Reting Monastery, a Buddhist monastery in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, and holders of the title have at times acted as the selector of a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>. The first, Ngawang Chokden, lived from 1677 to 1751. Thubten Jamphel Yeshe Gyaltsen was the fifth in the line, which had passed before him through Lobsang Yeshe Tenpa Rabgye, Ngawang Yeshe Tsultrim Gyaltsen and Ngawang Lobsang Yeshe Tenpai Gyaltsen. He held the office of regent, or desi, for the Dalai Lama whose search he had led.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He was replaced as regent in 1941, and was later alleged to have organised an uprising against his successor. He died in 1947 in the prisons of the Potala in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, apparently poisoned. A jailor allegedly reported that he had been tortured to death. The historian Melvyn C. Goldstein records the words of Tsepon W. D. Shakabpa, who said that many people believed this, but an investigation of the body was made for the Tibetan Assembly by a committee of Shakabpa, Tsarong, Khenchen Lobsang Tashi, Gyetakba and representatives of Reting and Sera Monasteries, and Tsarong told the Assembly there was no evidence that Reting had been strangled and no wound.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The affair exposed the politics of the religious hierarchy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>. His critics accused him of widespread corruption and, though a monk, of involvement with married women. Defenders held that his imprisonment owed something to his attraction to the teachings of the Nyingma lineage, a politically sensitive leaning, and that the case against him had been fabricated by the cabinet minister Kapshopa. Martin Scorsese's film Kundun (1997) tells of his regency, imprisonment and death. His reincarnation, Tenzin Jigme Thutob Wangchuk, was born in Lhasa in 1948 and enthroned in 1955.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Reting Rinpoche” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 26 January 1940 the Regent <b>Reting Rinpoche</b> asked the Chinese central government to exempt him from the lot-drawing with the Golden Urn, which it approved, and after a ransom of 400,000 silver dragons was paid to Ma Bufang he travelled to Lhasa and was enthroned at the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet (1912-1951) · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
